import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as r,q as s,f as d}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1839,"xkLessons":{"A.11":{"title":"A.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (Complete Interface Card)","lead":"Retain actual failure and unknown outcomes, and closely examine refusal and exit. Effectiveness is not voluntariness, and sacrifice does not prove love. The ending checks the non-authorization boundaries of all eleven variables together.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4314–4403, original-site location /theory/appendix-a/a-11#section-a-11. The current full text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Short quotation translated from Chinese: “It does not diagnose whether someone has love.” HV11 observes only evidenced actions, real costs, direction, voluntariness, alternative explanations, and consequences. It specializes H6/H2 and is constrained by N4; it does not score love by the amount of sacrifice or treat refusal as moral failure. The name must not be used to requisition the actor’s actions in reverse. [T-main]","HV11-R0-action-cost-record may still record candidate undertakings, cost distributions, coercion risks, gaps in stopping rights, and protective needs when action costs are grounded but voluntariness, refusal, or exit are insufficiently supported. What pauses is R1’s naming of open-ended undertaking, not the retention of known costs. Card requirements cannot force actors into refusal or disclosure they cannot safely perform. [T-main]","HV11-R1-voluntary-limited-action requires real action costs and a clear direction, with separate support for voluntariness, genuine refusal, and conditions for exit or negotiation, and checking relevant alternatives such as fear, dependency, interests, and performance. Outcomes are separately recorded as achieved, not achieved, harmful, or unknown. Genuine failed attempts are not erased because they did not succeed, nor does grounded voluntariness disappear because outcomes are unknown. [T-main]","HV11-R2-structural-consequence additionally requires eligible G2 identification of an above-threshold effect through the specified channel on preselected outcomes, plus CAUSAL/E4. Coerced action can also be effective, so R2 cannot backfill R1’s voluntariness; bounded voluntariness is not automatically effective either. The three routes are not a bad-to-good ladder, still less do effects prove love, goodness, justification, or unlimited responsibility. [T-main]","Ethical naming remains constrained by GOV-14’s normative premises; reaching R2 cannot replace them. An action description is also not an individual or group obligation: one person voluntarily contributing once does not mean similar members all consent to ongoing burdens. Representative relations, cost spillovers, genuine exit, and proxy protection must be added separately on elevation. Human voluntariness and meaning diagnoses do not apply to nonhuman systems. [T-main；T-gov]","Observed costs include time, resources, opportunities, and physical and psychological costs, but this interface makes no personality or clinical diagnoses. Separate immediate contributions, ongoing depletion, and recovery lags; beneficiaries, dependents, and alternative uptake providers may all be affected. Retain positions unable to refuse or exit safely or obscured by moral pressure; silence cannot fill in voluntariness. [T-main]","The appendix-wide notes at the end of this lesson must also be read: N in the nine-axis SP means network topology, whereas explicit runtime N means normative premises N1–N5; they differ. Identity belongs to K; relational channels belong to inputs, carriers, or causal contracts; membership positions belong to affected/excluded/observation fields. None can impersonate a scale axis. Expanding A/X/O/I does not expand J. [T-main]","The common table specifies outputs for each of the eleven variables: HV01, candidate domains, boundaries, and supplementary-evidence needs; HV02, boundaries, interfaces, exclusions, and testing needs; HV03, anchors, heterogeneous expression, and comparisons; HV04, GC/GS/GE candidates and transitions; HV05, CV/RS, costs, capacity, stopping rights, and needs for additional resources and redistribution; HV06, connectivity, lags, losses, interruptions, costs, and uptake; HV07, acceptance, fields, execution, persistence, write-back, and procedural repair; HV08, candidate conditions, heterogeneity, masking, and risk reduction; HV09, loads, capacity, recovery, local overload, and load-reduction needs; HV10, components, prototypes, mixtures, bounded mechanisms/historical increments, independent X0, and uncertainty under the selected route; HV11, voluntariness, costs, direction, alternatives, consequences, and needs for stopping and protection. [T-main]","All are descriptions or requirements, not authorization for actual testing, load reduction, additional resources, repair, protection, responsibility attribution, advancement, or exit. Any real adjustment separately requires C12, explicit normative premises, valid J, and O. Even obvious protective needs require checking independently existing authority; this card cannot be an authorization document. Authorized rollback revokes undertaking labels and requirements, actually restores refusal, exit, records, and resource states, and verifies completion. [T-main]","NASA ASRS’s confidentiality page describes voluntary reporting, deidentification, and restricted incentives; the first item on its separate Caveats page explicitly excludes accident or criminal-activity reports from confidentiality and penalty-immunity protections. This is used to examine the distinction between an institution’s description of itself as voluntary and its concrete protective conditions. It does not infer that every reporter can genuinely refuse, characterize them as loving, or claim R1/R2 support under this framework. [P-ASRS；P-ASRS-C]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV11. Interpretation: the short identifier within the cardbook tracks the same interface; it does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV11. Interpretation: the namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Open-ended undertaking action. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: Open-ended undertaking observes only real costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, and structural consequences; it does not diagnose whether someone has love. Interpretation: read this proposition together with the subsequent conditional routes and ceilings; do not extract an unconditional causal or normative conclusion from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Undertakings in human relationships, organizations, institutions, and public action. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: When voluntariness, refusal, or exit cannot be safely confirmed, pause R1’s naming of open-ended undertaking; R0 may still register candidate actions, costs, coercion risks, gaps in stopping rights, and protective needs. Personality and love diagnoses are always prohibited. Interpretation: inability to confirm voluntariness safely pauses R1 naming, while R0 retains costs, coercion risks, and protective needs; diagnosing love is always prohibited. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: Inferential dependencies provide the upstream content needed to establish a proposition. Upstream candidate/supported status must be carried through unchanged; citation does not automatically upgrade it. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card: 1. N4; 2. EVIDENCE; 3. SOURCE. Interpretation: protocol dependencies constrain evidence gathering and expression; completing protocols does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: 1. H6; 2. H2. Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV11-R0-action-cost-record; claim_level=normative_boundary; when=Actions and relevant costs are grounded, and known or unknown states of direction, benefits, and consequences can be recorded, but voluntariness, refusal, or exit are insufficiently supported.; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Describe candidate undertaking actions, cost distributions, coercion risks, gaps in stopping rights, and protective needs.; result_ceiling=Do not call this open-ended undertaking, diagnose love or personality, or demand continued undertaking. 2. route_id=HV11-R1-voluntary-limited-action; claim_level=normative_boundary; when=Actions and real costs are grounded, direction is clear, voluntariness, genuine refusal, and conditions for exit or negotiation are separately supported, and relevant alternatives are checked; record outcomes as achieved, not achieved, harmful, or unknown, without making structural success an entry condition.; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Register descriptions of bounded voluntary undertaking and outcome states; failure or unknown outcomes do not cancel grounded facts of action and voluntariness.; result_ceiling=Action description only; do not elevate individual undertaking into group obligations or authorize requisition, protection, or resource arrangements. 3. route_id=HV11-R2-structural-consequence; claim_level=conditional_effect; when=An eligible G2-instance shows that the action produces an above-threshold effect through the specified channel on preselected structural outcomes.; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4; allowed_conclusion=Register structural consequences of the action within the specified channel, object, and window.; result_ceiling=Structural effects prove neither voluntariness, love, goodness, justification, unlimited responsibility, nor action authorization; R2 effect support cannot supply missing R1 evidence, and coerced action may also have identifiable effects. Interpretation: gather evidence separately for R0 candidate costs, R1 bounded voluntariness, and R2 structural effects; effective coercion cannot supply voluntariness, and genuine failure is not absence of action. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. Describe bounded undertaking actions and consequences. Interpretation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached and with what strength remain limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. Diagnosing whether love exists; 2. Sacrifice equals love; 3. Responsibility equals unlimited undertaking; 4. Refusal equals moral failure. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: individual undertaking actions, actor cases, relationship or organizational populations, distributions of costs and voluntariness, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: relationship and care sites, organizational boundaries, digital public spaces, and cross-domain beneficiary scope; T=time span: immediate action, ongoing undertaking, depletion, recovery, and intergenerational windows; O=organizational level: action roles, relationships or teams, organizations, institutions, through governance ecosystems; C=causal level: undertaking events, action–cost interaction mechanisms, meso-level relational structures, institutional responsibility arrangements, and systemic conditions; R=observational resolution: original actions and costs, undertaking sequences, actor cases, distributions of costs and voluntariness, consequence indicators, and summaries, with compression loss registered; I=sphere of influence: direct actors and beneficiaries, dependents, indirect alternative uptake providers, second-order spillovers, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network-topological scope: dependency and care, beneficiary connections, alternative uptake, exit paths, and cross-domain cost networks; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for undertaking requirements, resource use, refusal, stopping, protection, and remedies; retain differences in voluntariness, costs, and exit. Interpretation: register all nine axes individually; particularly, sphere of influence I creates no jurisdictional authority J, and lower observational resolution R requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: Human undertaking arrangements requiring separate examination of actions, costs, direction, voluntariness, refusal conditions, and consequences; judge R0, R1, and R2 under their own conditions, without first filtering actions by structural consequences. Interpretation: this describes the interface’s corresponding object; choosing a lower-threshold descriptive route cannot treat functional meaning in the name as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. Costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, consequences, and stopping rights. Interpretation: invariants are comparative responsibilities retained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions on elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Distribution of voluntariness; 2. Representative relations; 3. Cost spillovers; 4. Genuine exit and proxy protection. Interpretation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Forms of undertaking, cost positions, and beneficiaries may change. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Inapplicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Nonhuman systems without capability for intention, voluntariness, responsibility, or meaning. Interpretation: inapplicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. Elevating individual undertaking into group obligations; 2. Transferring human undertaking concepts into the nonhuman core. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: 1. Candidate; 2. Voluntary and bounded; 3. Coercion risk; 4. One-sided depletion; 5. Stopping or exit. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Time, resources, opportunities, and physical and psychological costs invested in action; 2. Whether refusal, exit, stopping, and renegotiation are genuinely available; 3. Action direction, beneficiary positions, and detectable structural consequences; 4. Alternative explanations such as coercion, fear, dependency, interests, and performance. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. Actions and costs; 2. Refusal and exit conditions; 3. Alternative explanations; 4. Benefits and consequences. Interpretation: obtain listed evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, uncollected, inapplicable, and unavailable for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill a table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Orientation anchors; 2. Uptake layers; 3. Conditional potential fields; 4. Power and safety. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flow (output_effects). Original card: 1. Cost distributions; 2. Relational and institutional states; 3. Stopping and repair. Interpretation: variable flow identifies downstream judgments that may depend on this interface; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register immediate costs, ongoing undertaking, depletion, and recovery lags. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record dependency, fear, hidden coercion, and safety of expression. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Positions unable to refuse safely, unable to exit, or obscured by moral pressure. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Actors; 2. Beneficiaries; 3. Dependents; 4. Alternative uptake providers. Interpretation: register directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Concrete actors; 2. Relational practices; 3. Care or responsibility arrangements. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Those making demands; 2. Authorizers; 3. Those responsible as beneficiaries; 4. Those responsible for remedies. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: Constrained by N4; cannot be commanded or requisitioned. Interpretation: N4 prohibits commanding or requisitioning this ethical designation; independently grounded responsibilities cannot be expanded without limit by this label. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: Limited separately under R0/R1/R2: R0 describes only costs, direction, risks, and candidate actions; R1 describes actions in terms of voluntariness, refusal, and exit conditions under corresponding evidence; R2 makes bounded structural-consequence judgments only when corresponding G2 and causal conditions hold. No level may enter personality diagnosis, prove love or goodness, infer undertaking obligations, or generate real authorization. Ethical naming is separately constrained by GOV-14’s normative premises; reaching R2 cannot replace this requirement. Interpretation: no route proves love, personality, goodness, or real authorization; ethical naming additionally requires GOV-14 normative premises. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable produces only descriptions of voluntariness, real costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection; it authorizes no protective measures, undertaking demands, resource requisition, or personality verdicts. Any real adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: the interface itself creates no real-world authority to impose dispositions; actual actions separately require checking choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protections, and periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. One-sided sacrifice without the ability to refuse is praised as love or responsibility; 2. An undertaking claim lacks evidence of the claimed action or costs. Genuine failed attempts and unknown outcomes are recorded separately; their action descriptions cannot all be revoked for lacking structural success. Interpretation: counterexamples restrict unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or gathering evidence anew, without automatically negating every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, actors may refuse representation through safely accessible channels protected against retaliation, explain coercion, costs, alternative explanations, and exit restrictions, and trigger review independent of the original undertaking judgment or demand chain. Interpretation: appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not mean authority to correct errors exists. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must revoke undertaking labels and related demands within the specified period, actually restore refusal, exit, records, and resource states, and preserve versions and completion verification. Interpretation: actually cancel erroneous effects within valid authorization, correct downstream uses, and verify completion; changing document wording does not restore rights and resources to the affected person. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Check action costs and direction; where voluntariness cannot be safely checked, stop R1 naming while retaining R0 risks.","Separately check voluntariness, genuine refusal, exit or negotiation, and alternative explanations.","Record outcomes as achieved/not achieved/harmful/unknown; failure does not delete grounded actions.","R2 structural consequences separately require G2 and CAUSAL/E4; effects cannot fill R1 gaps.","Add representation, distributions, spillovers, and protections across scales; distinguish nine-axis N from normative N.","The eleven variables’ outputs generate requirements only; actual actions separately require C12/N/J/O checks and can be independently corrected."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Bounded voluntariness","leftText":"Actions and conditions for refusal and exit are grounded.","rightTitle":"Structural success","rightText":"Whether the preselected outcome was achieved requires separate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Effective action","leftText":"Identifiable effects may exist.","rightTitle":"Voluntary action","rightText":"Coercion can also be effective; the inference cannot run backward."},{"leftTitle":"A genuine failed attempt","leftText":"Action costs are grounded but the outcome was not achieved.","rightTitle":"An unsupported claim","rightText":"The claimed action or costs lack support; these are different cases."},{"leftTitle":"Network axis N","leftText":"Topological scope.","rightTitle":"Normative N","rightText":"N1–N5 choice premises; they cannot be entered interchangeably."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: one-sided sacrifice without ability to refuse is praised as love or responsibility.","Original-card counterexample: undertaking claims lack grounds for action costs; genuine failures and unknown outcomes must be recorded separately.","Teaching counterexample: one member voluntarily takes a shift, and everyone is then required to take shifts forever.","Boundary in the original: R2 effects cannot replace R1 voluntariness or GOV-14 premises for ethical naming."],"questions":["Can real undertaking be registered when structural outcomes are unknown?","Does greater sacrifice mean greater love?","Can effectiveness establish voluntariness?","Does a safety need automatically authorize me to provide protection?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"NASA ASRS explanations of voluntary reporting and institutional protection","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"NASA ASRS explanations of voluntary reporting and institutional protection","text":"The ASRS confidentiality page separately explains voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives.","sourceLabel":"NASA ASRS: Confidentiality and Incentives to Report","sourceUrl":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","sourceLocator":"Body text on voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives, webpage lines 30–32","boundary":"Only institutional arrangements and data limitations; it does not represent every reporter’s conditions of freedom or diagnose personality or love.","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.11","start_line":4407,"end_line":4496,"startLine":4407,"endLine":4496}],"outputId":"section-a-11","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: failure does not erase action","text":"A constructed volunteer spends two hours preparing an event under arrangements allowing safe refusal and exit; weather cancels the event, so the intended activity does not occur. The two hours of action and supported voluntary conditions may still be described, with nonachievement recorded separately; success cannot be invented to call it “undertaking,” nor can love be diagnosed.","assumptions":"All events are fictional; safe refusal and exit are stipulated solely for teaching.","boundary":"Actual R1 requires independent materials; a voluntary declaration alone does not pass it."},{"title":"Teaching construction: effective coercion still cannot become voluntary","text":"A constructed worker works overtime under threat of losing basic resources, and service output does improve. Even if a qualified G2 later supports that effect, it does not supply R1 voluntariness, refusal, or exit; retain the coercion risk and needs for stopping and protection.","assumptions":"The threat and outcome are fictional, separating effects from voluntariness without certifying real causation.","boundary":"Effects do not justify requiring continued undertaking; actual protection still requires independently valid authority and procedures."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: one-sided sacrifice without ability to refuse is praised as love or responsibility.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: undertaking claims lack grounds for action costs; genuine failures and unknown outcomes must be recorded separately.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: one member voluntarily takes a shift, and everyone is then required to take shifts forever.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: R2 effects cannot replace R1 voluntariness or GOV-14 premises for ethical naming.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can real undertaking be registered when structural outcomes are unknown?","answer":"When other R1 conditions are supported, the fact of voluntary action may be retained while the outcome is recorded as unknown.","basis":"Success in outcomes is not an entry requirement."},{"question":"Does greater sacrifice mean greater love?","answer":"Such a diagnosis must not be made; costs, voluntariness, coercion, and consequences are examined separately.","basis":"N4 boundary."},{"question":"Can effectiveness establish voluntariness?","answer":"No. Coerced action may also be effective; R2 does not supply R1.","basis":"Separation of the three routes."},{"question":"Does a safety need automatically authorize me to provide protection?","answer":"The variable only identifies needs; real measures require checks of C12/N/J/O and existing independent authority.","basis":"The shared action boundary of the eleven variables."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-routes.png","alt":"R0: supported cost candidates; R1: bounded voluntary action; R2: specified structural effects. They cannot compensate for one another.","caption":"Based on the three routes in A.11."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-results.png","alt":"Achieved, unachieved, harmful, and unknown outcomes are separate from voluntariness; failure does not erase supported action.","caption":"Based on A.11 R1/R2; the example is fictional."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/figures/hv11-common.png","alt":"The eleven variables generate only descriptions, gaps, and needs; distinguish nine-axis N from normative N, and separately check C12/N/J/O for real action.","caption":"Based on the appendix-wide shared explanation after A.11."}],"diagramBrief":"The three figures respectively show the three routes, two-dimensional separation of outcomes and voluntariness, and the shared non-authorizing boundary of the eleven variables.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No voluntariness, personality, or moral diagnosis was made of any real actor.","ASRS institutional explanations cannot establish safe exit in every case, and the first Caveats item explicitly states confidentiality exceptions."],"taskId":"S-A-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.11, Chinese source lines 4228–4317","claim_support":["All 39 HV11 fields and three routes, the concluding nine-axis explanation, and the output table for the eleven variables."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-gov","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§16.14, Chinese source lines 3458–3471","claim_support":["GOV-14 normative boundaries for the ethical naming of open-ended undertaking."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ASRS","title":"NASA ASRS: Confidentiality and Incentives to Report","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Webpage version at access","locator":"Body text on voluntary submission, confidential de-identification, and restricted incentives, webpage lines 30–32","claim_support":["ASRS explains confidential de-identification arrangements and restricted incentives for voluntary reports."],"limits":["The webpage body was read; institutional explanations on the website do not establish that every reporter can actually refuse, and do not constitute unlimited immunity or this framework’s R1/R2 certification."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-ASRS-C","title":"NASA ASRS: Caveats","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/caveats.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Webpage version at access","locator":"First body-text item and explanation of sanction limitations, webpage lines 29–31","claim_support":["Confidentiality and immunity from sanctions do not cover reports of accidents or criminal activities; on receipt, these are forwarded with names to the appropriate agencies. Immunity from sanctions has further limitations."],"limits":["The specified body text was read; this topic does not give current legal advice or describe limited institutional protection as absolute confidentiality."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking Action (Complete Interface Card); Chinese source lines 4314–4403; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/source/S-A-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"3576dd4c4e963de5668922749f54a2bb121ab66ab707271787260f19622bd210","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-11.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,f,m){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.11 HV11 Open-Ended Undertaking (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-11}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Retain actual failure and unknown outcomes, and closely examine refusal and exit. Effectiveness is not voluntariness, and sacrifice does not prove love. The ending checks the non-authorization boundaries of all eleven variables together.</span><details class="xk-gist__codes"><summary>Codes used in this section (16)</summary><ul><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-11"><b>HV11</b>Open-Ended Undertaking</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-4"><b>G2</b>Specified-Channel Effects and Per-Dimension Boundedness</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-8-4"><b>E4</b>Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-16.html#section-16-14"><b>GOV-14</b>Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-14-1"><b>C12</b>Check on the Validity of Normative Bridging</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-1"><b>HV01</b>Structural Domain</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-2"><b>HV02</b>Boundaries and Interfaces</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-3"><b>HV03</b>Orientation Anchor</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-4"><b>HV04</b>Generative Node</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-5"><b>HV05</b>Action Uptake Layer</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-6"><b>HV06</b>Drive–Uptake Chain</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-7"><b>HV07</b>Feedback Write-Back</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-8"><b>HV08</b>Conditional Potential Field</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-9"><b>HV09</b>Structural Load</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-10"><b>HV10</b>Evolutionary Phase</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-5"><b>X0</b>Orderly Exit and Benign Demise</a></li></ul></details></div><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV11</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV11</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Open-Ended Undertaking</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>Open-ended undertaking examines only actual costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, and structural consequences. It does not diagnose whether someone has love.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Undertaking in human relationships, organizations, institutions, and public action</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Suspend naming the action as open-ended undertaking under R1 if voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit cannot be confirmed safely. R0 may still register candidate actions, costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs. Diagnosing personality or love is always prohibited.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. N4<br>2. EVIDENCE<br>3. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>1. H6<br>2. H2</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV11-R0-action-cost-record;<br>claim_level=normative_boundary;<br>when=There are grounds for the action and its costs. Direction, who benefits, and the consequences can each be recorded as known or unknown, but evidence of voluntariness or the ability to refuse or exit is still insufficient;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Describe candidate undertakings, the distribution of costs, coercion risks, gaps in the right to stop, and protection needs;<br>result_ceiling=Do not call the action open-ended undertaking, diagnose love or personality, or require the person to continue.<br>2. route_id=HV11-R1-voluntary-limited-action;<br>claim_level=normative_boundary;<br>when=There are grounds for the action and its actual costs, its direction is clear, and voluntariness and genuine conditions for refusal, exit, or negotiation receive separate support. Relevant alternative explanations are checked. Record the outcome as realized, unrealized, harmful, or unknown. Structural success is not required for entry;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a description of bounded voluntary undertaking and the outcome status. Failure or an unknown outcome does not cancel supported facts about the action and its voluntariness;<br>result_ceiling=Description of the action only. Do not elevate individual undertaking to a group duty or authorize conscription, protection, or resource arrangements.<br>3. route_id=HV11-R2-structural-consequence;<br>claim_level=conditional_effect;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance shows that the action has an above-threshold effect on the preselected structural outcome through the specified channel;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register the action’s structural consequences within the specified channel, object, and window;<br>result_ceiling=A structural effect does not establish voluntariness, love, goodness, normative justification, unlimited responsibility, or authorization to act. Support for an effect in R2 cannot fill an evidence gap in R1. Coerced action can also have identifiable effects.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Description of bounded undertaking and its consequences</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Diagnosing whether someone has love<br>2. Equating sacrifice with love<br>3. Equating responsibility with unlimited undertaking<br>4. Equating refusal with moral failure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual acts of undertaking, cases involving actors, populations of relationships or organizations, distributions of costs and voluntariness, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers sites of relationships and care, organizational boundaries, digital public spaces, and the reach of benefits across domains. T=Time span covers windows for immediate action, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, recovery, and effects across generations. O=Organizational level covers roles in action, relationships or teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers events of undertaking, mechanisms of interaction between actions and costs, meso-level relational structures, institutional arrangements of responsibility, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original actions and costs, sequences of undertaking, cases involving actors, distributions of costs and voluntariness, consequence metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers direct actors and beneficiaries, dependents, those indirectly taking over, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers dependencies in care, links of benefit, alternative arrangements for taking over, exit paths, and cost networks across domains. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for demands to take up work, resource use, refusal, stopping, protection, and remedy. Preserve differences in voluntariness, costs, and exit</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>Human arrangements for undertaking in which action, cost, direction, voluntariness, conditions for refusal, and consequences must be examined separately. Judge R0, R1, and R2 under their own conditions without first screening actions by their structural consequences</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Costs, voluntariness, direction, alternative explanations, consequences, and rights to stop</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Distribution of voluntariness<br>2. Relations of representation<br>3. Cost spillovers<br>4. A genuine means of exit and protection provided through representatives</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Forms of undertaking, positions bearing costs, and beneficiaries can change</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Nonhuman systems without intentionality, voluntariness, responsibility, or the capability for meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Elevating individual undertaking to a group duty<br>2. Transferring the concept of human undertaking into the nonhuman core</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Candidate<br>2. Voluntary and bounded<br>3. Risk of coercion<br>4. Unilateral exhaustion<br>5. Stopping or exit</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Time and resources invested, opportunities forgone, and bodily and psychological costs of the action<br>2. Whether refusal, exit, stopping, and renegotiation are genuinely available<br>3. Direction of the action, positions receiving benefits, and detectable structural consequences<br>4. Alternative explanations such as coercion, fear, dependence, interests, and acting for show</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Actions and costs<br>2. Conditions for refusal and exit<br>3. Alternative explanations<br>4. Benefits and consequences</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Orientation anchor<br>2. Uptake layer<br>3. Conditional potential field<br>4. Power and safety</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Distribution of costs<br>2. States of relationships and institutions<br>3. Stopping and repair</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Register immediate costs, sustained undertaking, exhaustion, and recovery lags</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record dependence, fear, hidden coercion, and safety of expression</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Positions where people cannot refuse safely, cannot exit, or are masked by moral pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. Actors<br>2. Beneficiaries<br>3. Dependents<br>4. People taking over</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Specific actors<br>2. Relational practices<br>3. Arrangements for care or responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Those making demands<br>2. Parties granting authorization<br>3. Parties responsible as beneficiaries<br>4. Parties responsible for remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>Subject to N4. It cannot be commanded or conscripted</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Apply the limits for R0, R1, and R2 separately. R0 describes only costs, direction, risks, and candidate actions. With corresponding evidence, R1 describes the action’s voluntariness and conditions for refusal and exit. R2 judges structural consequences within a limited scope only if the corresponding G2 and causal conditions hold. No level may diagnose personality, prove love or goodness, infer a duty to undertake, or authorize real-world action. Ethical naming is separately subject to the normative premises of GOV-14. Reaching R2 does not satisfy that requirement.</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of voluntariness, actual costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection. It does not authorize protective measures, demands to take up work, requisition of resources, or adjudication of personality. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. One-sided sacrifice that cannot be refused is praised as love or responsibility<br>2. A claim of undertaking lacks evidence of the stated action or cost. Actual failed attempts and unknown outcomes are recorded separately. Their action descriptions must not be categorically withdrawn for lack of structural success</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, actors can refuse to be represented and report coercion, costs, alternative explanations, and restrictions on exit through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original judgment or chain of demands concerning the undertaking</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke the undertaking designation and related demands and actually restore the conditions of refusal and exit and the state of records and resources. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><code>scale_profile</code> is the complete nine-axis record SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A single “micro–macro” label cannot stand in for it. A is aggregation level, which must declare units, population, distribution, and aggregation rules. X covers physical space and digital boundaries. T covers windows, lags, and cycles. O is organizational level across roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C is causal level across events, interaction mechanisms, meso-level structures, institutions, and systemic conditions. R is resolution across original events, sequences, individual cases, distributions, metrics, and summaries, with compression loss recorded. I is the scope of direct, indirect, second-order, cross-domain, and intergenerational impacts. N is network topology scope. J is scope of jurisdiction and authorization. Expanding A, X, O, or I does not automatically expand J. Observing more cannot confer greater authority to act.</p><p>Recorded identities and the object’s identity criterion belong to K in the object contract. Relationships and channels of effect belong in <code>input_dependencies</code>, <code>carrier</code>, or the causal contract. Membership and affected positions belong in <code>affected_positions</code>, <code>local_exclusion_zone</code>, and observation fields. None may substitute for a scale axis. The N scale axis is network topology. Below, “explicit N premises at runtime” refers to N1–N5 in the normative choice layer. The two must not be conflated.</p><p>All 11 variables share the same limit on action. The variables themselves produce only descriptions, evidence gaps, and requirements for action. They do not authorize real-world adjustments. Their output ceilings are as follows.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Descriptions or Needs This Variable Can Produce</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HV01</td><td>Candidate structural domains, boundary disputes, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>HV02</td><td>Boundary states, interface barriers, exclusion risks, and testing needs</td></tr><tr><td>HV03</td><td>Candidate anchors, diverse expressions, comparison results, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>HV04</td><td>Candidate classifications for GC, GS, and GE, state transitions, and needs for more evidence</td></tr><tr><td>HV05</td><td>CV, RS, costs, capacity, rights to stop, gaps in uptake, and needs to reduce load, add resources, or reallocate them</td></tr><tr><td>HV06</td><td>Chain connectivity, time lags, losses, interruptions, costs, and uptake needs</td></tr><tr><td>HV07</td><td>Intake, changes to record fields, execution, duration, gaps in write-back, and needs for procedural repair</td></tr><tr><td>HV08</td><td>Candidate channels through which conditions could have effects, differences across positions, masking of evidence, and needs to reduce risk</td></tr><tr><td>HV09</td><td>Load, capacity, recovery, local overload, and needs to reduce load or add resources</td></tr><tr><td>HV10</td><td>Component applicability, prototype matches, and mixed states under the selected route, limited explanations of phase mechanisms or historical conditional increments, independent X0 path status, uncertainty, and observation needs</td></tr><tr><td>HV11</td><td>Voluntariness, actual costs, direction, alternative explanations, structural consequences, and needs for stopping and protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>However obvious the need may seem, any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures. The variables must not authorize testing, load reduction, adding resources, repair, protection, attribution of responsibility, exploratory action, advancement, exit arrangements, or other action on their own.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.11"}),e[1]||(e[1]=d("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=n(c,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
